      "math_academy_id": "q-118546",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/926161e90964be4d6d4d32491ce4b6fdfa7e964d5d88ebc4eb7dd367dd2a837f.png)\n\nThe vectors $\\text{a},$ $\\text{b},$ and $\\text{c}$ are shown in the diagram above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $a-b+c.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\na-b=a+(-b).\n$$\n\nThe resultant $a-b$ is the vector that joins the tail of $\\text{a}$ and the head of $-\\text{b}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/a487c6406c591962ce27be6c27006c2d8f2ef49d05ee704007d578788db77883.png)\n\nNow, we use the triangle law of addition to find \n\n$$\n(\\text{a}-\\text{b})+\\text{c}.\n$$\n\nWe draw $\\text{c}$ from the head of $\\text{a}-\\text{b}.$ So, we have the vector that goes from the tail of $\\text{a}$ to the head of $\\text{c}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/122df7ad9d20a94049597c1a75adf75f8286b7e67ca9b9a661029e21fea6393c.png)",
